     1	# Few-shot Acoustic Synthesis with Multimodal Flow Matching
     2	Training and inference code for the paper "Few-shot Acoustic Synthesis with Multimodal Flow Matching", accepted at CVPR 2026.
     3	
     4	<p align="center" style="margin: 2em auto;">
     5	    <a href='https://amandinebtto.github.io/FLAC' style='padding-left: 0.5rem;'><img src='https://img.shields.io/badge/FLAC-Project_page-orange?style=flat&logo=github&logoColor=orange' alt='Project Page'></a>
     6	    <a href='https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.19176'><img src='https://img.shields.io/badge/arXiv-Paper-red?style=flat&logo=arXiv&logoColor=green' alt='arXiv'></a>
     7	    <a href="https://huggingface.co/AmandineBtto/FLAC" target="_blank"><img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/%F0%9F%A4%97%20Hugging%20Face-FLAC_Weights-blue" alt="Weights"></a>
     8	    <a href="https://huggingface.co/AmandineBtto/AGREE" target="_blank"><img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/%F0%9F%A4%97%20Hugging%20Face-AGREE_Weights-blue" alt="Weights"></a>
     9	</p>
    10	
    11	
    12	This repository includes:
    13	- **FLAC**: our method for one-shot room impulse response generation.
    14	- **AGREE**: a CLIP-style acoustic–geometry embedding model introduced for evaluating geometry-consistenty in RIR generation and enabling downstream tasks. 
    15	
    16	
    17	## Method Overview
    18	
    19	![Overview](assets/concept.png)
    20	
    21	## Install
    22	Clone this repository, navigate to the root, and run:
    23	```bash
    24	conda create -n flac python=3.10
    25	conda activate flac
    26	pip install .
    27	```
    28	
    29	**Hardware Requirements**
    30	- Evaluation & Finetuning: All evaluation, HAA finetuning, and AGREE training can be performed on an RTX 4090 (24GB).
    31	- FLAC Training: Training the VAE and the main FLAC model with EMA requires an H100 (80GB).
    32	- Flash Attention: Requires PyTorch >= 2.5. We use version 2.7.4.post1. Refer to the [Flash Attention GitHub repo](https://github.com/Dao-AILab/flash-attention) for troubleshooting.
    33	
    34	
    35	## Quick Start: Inference
    36	To quickly generate RIRs and evaluate FLAC using pretrained weights
    37	1. Download weights: run `bash download_weights.sh` in the repo root.
    38	2. Download the [AcousticRooms dataset](https://github.com/facebookresearch/AcousticRooms).
    39	3. Run:
    40	```bash
    41	python eval_FLAC.py \
    42	--model-config src/configs/model_configs/FLAC/AR/FLAC_AR.json \
    43	--dataset-config src/configs/dataset_configs/AR/eval/acousticroom_seeneval.json \
    44	--ckpt-path weights/FLAC/FLAC_EMA.ckpt \
    45	--cfg-scale 1.0 \
    46	--steps 1 \
    47	--eval-name eval_FLAC \
    48	--store_predictions
    49	```
    50	
    51	## Global Repository Structure
    52	```
    53	.
    54	├── AGREE/               # AGREE model & training (Run AGREE commands from here)
    55	├── assets/              # README images
    56	├── baselines/           # Baseline heuristics (KNN, etc.)
    57	├── data/                # Dataset JSON splits and processing script (HAA)
    58	├── src/                 # Core FLAC code
    59	│   ├── configs/         # All .json configurations
    60	│   ├── ...
    61	├── weights/             # Model checkpoints (Download here)
    62	├── download_weights.sh  # Script to download weights from Hugging Face
    63	└── *.py                 # Main entry points (train.py, eval_*.py)
    64	```
    65	
    66	## Datasets Preparation
    67	
    68	### AcousticRooms (AR)
    69	Instructions for downloading the dataset are available [here](https://github.com/facebookresearch/AcousticRooms).
    70	
    71	Unlike the original xRIR setup which trains separate models for evaluation on seen/unseen sets, we train a single model for evaluation on both. We modified the original split to ensure zero overlap between the training set and evaluation sets. Use the updated files in `data/AR/`.
    72	
    73	
    74	### HAA
    75	Download from [Zenodo](https://zenodo.org/records/11195833) the Hearing Anything Anywhere (HAA).
    76	
    77	We only use the 'base' configurations for each of the four room categories (classroom, complex, dampened, hallway).
    78	For each room, you only need `RIRs.npy` and `xyzs.npy`.
    79	
    80	We provide depth maps at the source positions in: `data/HAA/depth`. Move these into your dataset directory so each room has a `depth_images/` subfolder (e.g., path/to/HAA/classroomBase/depth_images/classroomBase_depth_image.npy).
    81	The depth maps were generated using the surface annotations available [here](https://github.com/maswang32/hearinganythinganywhere/tree/main/rooms). The process was: (i) create a mesh from the surface annotations; (ii) Render a panoramic depth map at each source position.
    82	
    83	To convert the dataset into the required format (audio + poses), run the `prepare_data.py` script in `data/HAA`:
    84	```bash
    85	python prepare_data.py \
    86	--dataset-path /path/to/HAA/dataset \
    87	--output-dir /path/to/processed/HAA/dataset
    88	```
    89	
    90	For completeness, we provide the train/val/test split files used in our experiments in `data/HAA`, but they should be recreated by the `prepare_data.py` script.
    91	
    92	## Weights
    93	The weights for FLAC and AGREE can be downloaded from Hugging Face by running `download_weights.sh` in the repo root. Place the downloaded files in the `weights/` folder.
    94	
    95	
    96	### Model Unwrapping
    97	The checkpoint files created during training include the training wrapper, which greatly increases the size of the checkpoint file.
    98	`unwrap_model.py` takes in a wrapped model checkpoint and saves a new checkpoint file including only the model itself.
    99	
   100	That can be run with from the repo root with:
   101	```bash
   102	python unwrap_model.py \
   103	--model-config /path/to/model/config 
   104	--ckpt-path /path/to/wrapped/ckpt 
   105	--name model_unwrap
   106	--use-safetensors
   107	```
   108	
   109	## Training 
   110	
   111	### FLAC
   112	To start a training run on the AcousticRooms dataset, run the `train.py` script in the repo root with:
   113	```bash
   114	python train.py \
   115	--dataset-config src/configs/dataset_configs/AR/train/acousticroom_train.json \
   116	--val-dataset-config src/configs/dataset_configs/AR/eval/acousticroom_seeneval.json \
   117	--val-every 2500 \
   118	--checkpoint-every 2500 \
   119	--batch-size 64 \
   120	--num-workers 8 \
   121	--model-config src/configs/model_configs/FLAC/AR/FLAC_AR.json \
   122	--pretransform-ckpt-path weights/FLAC/VAE.safetensors \
   123	--save-dir ./outputs_FLAC \
   124	--experiment-name FLAC_training \
   125	--name FLAC
   126	```
   127	
   128	### VAE
   129	The VAE takes multiple days on an H100. We provide a pretrained checkpoint (3.08M steps). To train from scratch:
   130	```bash
   131	python train.py \
   132	--dataset-config src/configs/dataset_configs/AR/train/acousticroom_train.json \
   133	--val-dataset-config src/configs/dataset_configs/AR/eval/acousticroom_seeneval.json \
   134	--model-config src/configs/model_configs/VAE/VAE.json \
   135	--batch-size 64 \
   136	--num-workers 8 \
   137	--val-every 10000 \
   138	--save-dir ./outputs_VAE \
   139	--name VAE \
   140	--experiment-name VAE_training \
   141	```
   142	**Note**: Trained VAE checkpoints must be processed via `unwrap_model.py` before use in FLAC training.
   143	
   144	### Finetuning on HAA
   145	To finetune FLAC pretrained on AR on the HAA dataset, first change `max_steps` in `train.py` to 1000, then you can run:
   146	```bash
   147	python train.py \
   148	--dataset-config src/configs/dataset_configs/HAA/train/haa_train.json \
   149	--val-dataset-config src/configs/dataset_configs/HAA/eval/haa_val.json \
   150	--model-config src/configs/model_configs/FLAC/HAA/FLAC_HAA_finetune.json \
   151	--val-every 10 \
   152	--checkpoint-every 10 \
   153	--num-workers 8 \
   154	--batch-size 16 \
   155	--accum-batches 4 \
   156	--name FLAC_HAA \
   157	--pretransform-ckpt-path weights/FLAC/VAE.safetensors \
   158	--pretrained-ckpt-path weights/FLAC/FLAC_EMA.ckpt \
   159	```
   160	Because the dataset is small, we accumulate gradients over 4 batches to increase the effective batch size.
   161	
   162	**Performance Tip**: Removing the minus in `src/configs/dataset_configs/custom_metadata/HAA_md.py` (L70) improves acoustic metrics for HAA. This is consistent with the fact that the setup is reversed compared to AR (depth at the source position instead of the listener position). See the paper supplementary material for more details on this.
   163	
   164	**Paper Metrics**: To obtain the metrics presented in the paper, we averaged over individual results per scene. The evaluation script also provides a mean over all the samples from all the scenes, which is different.
   165	
   166	### FLAC Config Variants
   167	We provide the config for different models from our paper. 
   168	| Config File | Conditioning | Description |
   169	| ----------- | ------------ | ----------- |
   170	| `FLAC_AR.json` | AdaLN + Cross-Attn | Baseline. |
   171	| `FLAC_AR_S.json` | AdaLN + Cross-Attn | Smaller DiT (Depth of 4). Similar performances. |
   172	| `FLAC_AR_AllCA.json` | Cross-Attn| All conditioning via cross-attention. |
   173	| `FLAC_AR_InContext.json` | Prepended | Conditioning prepended to input sequence. |
   174	| `FLAC_AR_VAECtxt.json` | AdaLN + Cross-Attn | Uses VAE to encode context RIRs instead of ResNet18. Works with dataset config `acousticroom_train_VAECtxt.json`|
   175	| `FLAC_AR_noGeom.json` | AdaLN + Cross-Attn | Evaluates FLAC without geometric conditioning (ablation).|
   176	
   177	
   178	## Evaluation
   179	
   180	### FLAC
   181	Evaluate using either the standard script or the PyTorch Lightning wrapper:
   182	
   183	```bash
   184	# Standard script
   185	python eval_FLAC.py \
   186	--model-config /path/to/model/config \
   187	--dataset-config /path/to/eval/dataset/config \
   188	--cfg-scale 1.0 \
   189	--steps 1 \
   190	--eval-name eval_FLAC \
   191	--ckpt-path /path/to/FLAC/ckpt
   192	
   193	# PyTorch Lightning wrapper
   194	python eval_pl.py \ 
   195	--model-config /path/to/model/config \ 
   196	--val-dataset-config /path/to/eval/dataset/config  \
   197	--cfg-scale 1.0 
   198	--steps 1 
   199	--name FLAC
   200	--ckpt-path /path/to/FLAC/ckpt
   201	--store-predictions False
   202	```
   203	
   204	**Reduced Acoustic Context**: To evaluate FLAC with a lower number of context RIRs, use the appropriate config files in `src/configs/dataset_configs`.
   205	
   206	### Baselines
   207	We provide a unified evaluation script that supports multiple baseline methods. The baselines serve as comparison points for learned generative models by using simple heuristics based on spatial proximity or random selection.
   208	
   209	The following baseline methods are supported:
   210	- RdnAcross: Randomly samples a RIR from the entire dataset. This baseline ignores all spatial information and serves as a lower bound on performance.
   211	- RdnSame: Randomly samples a RIR from the same room/scene as the target. This baseline uses room identity but ignores the specific source-receiver positions.
   212	- KNN: K-Nearest Neighbor approach that selects the reference RIR whose source position is closest to the target source position. This baseline uses explicit spatial reasoning with `--num-shot` reference examples.
   213	- LinearInterp: Linear interpolation of multiple reference RIRs weighted by inverse distance to the target source position. Uses `--num-shot` reference examples and performs weighted interpolation in the waveform domain.
   214	
   215	The `eval_baselines.py` script handles both AR and HAA datasets:
   216	
   217	```bash
   218	# AR (KNN 8-shot unseen evaluation)
   219	python -m baselines.eval_baselines \
   220	--dataset AR \
   221	--baseline KNN \
   222	--num-shot 8 \
   223	--split unseen \
   224	--data-path /path/to/AR/dataset \
   225	--seen-json data/AR/seen_eval.json \
   226	--ckpt-AGREE weights/AGREE/AGREE_fullAR.pt \
   227	--output-dir baselines_results \
   228	
   229	# HAA (RdnAcross test evaluation)
   230	python -m baselines.eval_baselines \
   231	--dataset HAA \
   232	--baseline RdnAcross \
   233	--split-file data/HAA/test_base.json \
   234	--data-path /path/to/HAA_dataset \
   235	--ckpt-AGREE weights/AGREE/AGREE_fullHAA.pt \
   236	--out-dir baselines_results/
   237	```
   238	
   239	
   240	### VAE 
   241	To eval the VAE:
   242	
   243	```bash
   244	python eval_VAE.py \
   245	--model-config /path/to/model/config \
   246	--dataset-config /path/to/eval/dataset/config \
   247	--eval-name eval_VAE \
   248	--ckpt-path /path/to/VAE/ckpt
   249	```
   250	**Note**: You need to give the `.ckpt` file in that case, not the `.safetensors` file.
   251	
   252	## AGREE
   253	
   254	### Training & Finetuning
   255	To train AGREE on the AR dataset, run: 
   256	```bash
   257	cd AGREE
   258	python -m AGREE_train.main \
   259	--data-path /path/to/AcousticRooms/Dataset \
   260	--folder-name single_channel_ir_1 \
   261	--json-file-train-path ../data/AR/train.json \
   262	--json-file-val-path ../data/AR/seen_eval.json \
   263	--dataset-type AR \
   264	--model dinoV3 \
   265	--save-frequency 10 \
   266	--report-to wandb  
   267	--sample-size 10240 \
   268	--sample-rate 22050 \
   269	--warmup 10000 \
   270	--batch-size 64 \
   271	--workers=8 \
   272	--lr=1e-4 \
   273	--wd=0.1 \
   274	--epochs=100 \   
   275	--name AGREE_AR \ 
   276	--precision amp 
   277	```
   278	
   279	For finetuning on HAA, run:
   280	```bash
   281	cd AGREE
   282	python -m AGREE_train.main \
   283	--data-path /path/to/HAA/Dataset \
   284	--folder-name mono_rirs_22050Hz \
   285	--json-file-train-path ../data/HAA/alldata_base.json \
   286	--json-file-val-path ../data/HAA/val_base.json \
   287	--dataset-type HAA \
   288	--model dinoV3 \
   289	--save-frequency 10 \
   290	--report-to wandb  
   291	--sample-size 10240 \
   292	--sample-rate 22050 \
   293	--warmup 10000 \
   294	--batch-size 64 \
   295	--workers=8 \
   296	--lr=1e-4 \
   297	--wd=0.1 \
   298	--epochs=100 \   
   299	--name AGREE_HAA \ 
   300	--precision amp \
   301	--pretrained /path/to/AGREE_AR/ckpt
   302	```
   303	
   304	We also provide the config files for using xRIR ViT or OpenCLIP ViT-S/16 instead of DINOv3 (see ablation in the paper's supplementary material).
   305	
   306	We provide the AGREE checkpoints: 
   307	- `AGREE_fullAR.pt` / `AGREE_fullHAA.pt`: Use these only for evaluation (FD/Recall metrics) as they have seen the full dataset.
   308	- `AGREE_AR.pt`: Use this if you want to use AGREE as a pretrained backbone for further downstream tasks without data leakage.
   309	
   310	### Evaluation
   311	Run
   312	```bash
   313	python -m AGREE_train.main \
   314	--data-path /path/to/AR/Dataset \
   315	--json-file-val-path ../data/HAA/test_base.json \
   316	--folder-name mono_rirs_22050Hz_librosa \
   317	--dataset-type HAA \
   318	--sample-size 10240 \
   319	--sample-rate 22050 \
   320	--batch-size 128 \
   321	--workers=8 \
   322	--model dinoV3 \
   323	--precision amp \
   324	--pretrained ../weights/AGREE/AGREE_AR.pt
   325	```
